{ Our flagship Orthodox mainline: White preserves central tension until Black surrenders e5, then pivots into Anatoly Karpov's textbook e-file siege. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 $1 { 4. Bg5 is our non-negotiable starting gun. Developing the knight first lets Black hijack the move order into Ragozin or Vienna terrain, whereas 4. Bg5 immediately questions d5. } { [%csl Yg5] [%cal Bg5f6] } ( 4. Nf3 $6 Bb4 { — Move-order violation: allows Classical Ragozin and Botvinnik complexes we deliberately bypass. } ) 4... Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 Nbd7 7. Rc1 { 7. Rc1! waits patiently for Black to declare their hand before committing the f1 bishop. Developing Bd3 right away lets Black strike with ...c5 and win tempo. } { [%csl Yc1] [%cal Bg5h4,Bf1d3] } 7... c6 8. Bd3 dxc4 { 8... dxc4 accepts the positional challenge — White will recapture with tempo while aiming to neutralize Black's coming ...e5 break. If instead Black tries 8... a6, they enter a quiet Carlsbad. } { [%cal Bd3c4] } ( 8... a6 9. cxd5 exd5 10. O-O Re8 11. Qc2 { — Early ...a6 commits a target on the queenside and transposes directly into Line 1.4's Carlsbad Minority Attack. } ) 9. Bxc4 Nd5 { 9... Nd5 uses Capablanca's freeing maneuver to trade two pairs of minor pieces and ease Black's cramp. What if Black pushes 9... b5 immediately?: } { [%cal Bg5e7] } ( 9... b5 10. Bd3 { — Black weakens c5 and c6 prematurely; White keeps a comfortable +0.25 edge with smooth kingside development. } ) 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 { Exchange check: trading dark-squared bishops with 10. Bxe7 is virtually forced since moving the g5 bishop hands Black free tempos, while forcing the queen to e7 exposes it along the e-file once the center opens. } { [%csl Ye7] [%cal Bc4d5] } 11. O-O Nxc3 { On 11... Nxc3, recapturing 12. Rxc3! with the rook is vital — taking with the b-pawn would ruin our queenside structure and waste the c1 rook's semi-open file. Now the rook can lift along the third rank. } { [%csl Bc3] [%cal Bc1c3] } 12. Rxc3 e5 { 12... e5 is the principled break, fighting for central parity. If Black chickens out with 12... b6, White seizes an immediate structural bind: } { [%cal Re5d4] } ( 12... b6 13. Qc2 c5 14. dxc5 { — 14. dxc5! gives White a clear +0.82 to +0.87 structural grip against Black's queenside pawns. } ) 13. Bb3 $1 { 13. Bb3! — Karpov's signature retreat. We refuse to release tension on e5 because trading on e5 lets Black's c8 bishop step out effortlessly, whereas holding tension forces Black to declare the center. } { [%csl Yb3,Re5] [%cal Bb3c2,Gd4e5] } ( 13. dxe5 Nxe5 14. Nxe5 Qxe5 { — Premature simplification: Stockfish evaluates +0.03 to +0.07, and Black's bishop activates cleanly. } ) 13... exd4 14. exd4 { Exchange check: recapturing 14. exd4! rather than 14. Qxd4 — +0.11 opens the e-file for Re1 and keeps the queen flexible, giving us a dynamic d4 IQP at +0.38 to +0.49.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.38 to +0.45 — Dynamic d4 IQP with Open e-File Initiative<br><br>• Our Plan — Anchor Re1 and Ne5, then swing the c3 rook via Re3 or Rg3 toward the kingside; preserve the b3 bishop on the a2-g8 diagonal.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black will rush ...Nf6 to contest d5 and e4 and free the c8 bishop for ...Be6 or ...Qd6-f4 counterplay; keep f2-f4 and d4-d5 tactical strikes ready.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Keep queens and rooks for kingside pressure; if pieces come off, the c3 rook infiltrates down the e-file against f7 while our active kingside pawns and d4 outpost often decide the endgame.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>Explore Karpov's natural e-file continuations against Black's top practical replies: } { [%csl Gd4,Be5,Rf7] [%cal Bf1e1,Bf3e5,Bc3e3,Rd7f6] } ( 14. exd4 Nf6 15. Re1 Qd8 16. Ne5 { — Top engine and 97% human reply: White plants Ne5 with a rook lift to g3 and strong +0.38 to +0.44 initiative.<br><br>What if Black sidesteps with 14... Qd6 or 14... Nb6?: } ) ( 14. exd4 Qd6 15. Re1 Nf6 16. Ne5 { — Karpov's own target zone: Ne5 hits f7 and prepares Rg3 — +0.45, as seen in Karpov vs Campora.<br><br>And against 14... Nb6?: } ) ( 14. exd4 Nb6 15. Re1 Qd8 16. Re5 { — Leaving e5 unguarded lets White's rook invade e5 before swinging to g5 — +0.52. } ) *

