{ When Black inserts 8... h6 before ...dxc4 and ...b5, old 12. O-O lets Black clamp the queenside with ...c5 and ...c4. Precise 12. a4! keeps an honest edge. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 Nbd7 7. Rc1 c6 8. Bd3 h6 { 8... h6 forces our dark-squared bishop to commit to h4, after which Black can also try 8... Re8 before capturing on c4. } { [%cal Bg5h4] } ( 8... Re8 9. O-O dxc4 10. Bxc4 Nd5 11. Ne4 { — Against 8... Re8 9... dxc4 10... Nd5, skipping 11. Bxe7 in favor of immediate 11. Ne4! hits +0.78 and keeps huge practical pressure.<br><br>And if Black avoids ...dxc4 with 9... Nf8?: } ) ( 8... Re8 9. O-O Nf8 10. c5 b6 11. b4 { — White slams the door with c5 and b4 for a clear +1.04 queenside bind. } ) 9. Bh4 dxc4 { 9... dxc4 starts the direct queenside expansion. Check Black's two solid alternatives here — 9... a6 and 9... b6: } { [%cal Bd3c4] } ( 9... a6 10. c5 e5 11. dxe5 { — Against 9... a6, seizing space with 10. c5! and meeting 10... e5 with 11. dxe5 keeps +0.42.<br><br>And if 9... b6?: } ) ( 9... b6 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. O-O Bb7 { — 9... b6 10. cxd5 cxd5 opens the c-file cleanly where White enjoys smooth piece coordination — +0.43. } ) 10. Bxc4 b5 { 10... b5 kicks our bishop to gain space for ...Bb7 and ...c5. If Black drops into 10... Nd5 or 10... a6 first, we stay flexible: } { [%csl Gb5] [%cal Bc4d3] } ( 10... Nd5 11. Bg3 Nxc3 12. bxc3 { — Trading knights strengthens our center and opens the b-file for +0.13.<br><br>Against 10... a6?: } ) ( 10... a6 11. a4 Nd5 12. Bg3 { — Preventing ...b5 with 11. a4 keeps a clean +0.14 advantage. } ) 11. Bd3 a6 { 11... a6 prepares ...c5 and ...Bb7. If Black plays 11... Bb7 immediately, simple castling keeps +0.20. } { [%cal Ga2a4] } ( 11... Bb7 12. O-O { — 11... Bb7 delays ...a6; castling leaves White +0.20 ahead in development. } ) 12. a4 $1 { 12. a4! breaks the queenside chain right before Black can play ...c5 and ...c4. Natural castling here is the classic trap of the line — it hands Black full equality. } { [%csl Ya4,Rb5] [%cal Ga4b5] } ( 12. O-O $6 c5 13. a4 c4 { — Autopilot mistake — -0.08 to -0.11: Black locks the queenside with ...c4! and neutralizes our pressure completely. } ) 12... bxa4 { 12... bxa4 splits Black's queenside pawns cleanly. Look at Black's three alternatives — 12... Bb7, 12... b4, and 12... c5: } { [%cal Bc3a4] } ( 12... Bb7 13. O-O { — Keeping b5 under tension lets White castle smoothly — +0.21.<br><br>What if 12... b4?: } ) ( 12... b4 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. Ne4 { — Pushing past leaves weak dark squares on c5 and b6 — +0.61.<br><br>And 12... c5?: } ) ( 12... c5 13. axb5 axb5 14. Bxb5 { — Immediate 12... c5 drops the b5 pawn tactics cleanly — +0.44. } ) 13. Nxa4 { 13. Nxa4 leaves Black with isolated pawns on a6 and c6 while our knight eyes c5. Expect a queen check next move, which we block calmly.<br><br>━━━ PLAN FROM HERE ━━━<br><br>Verdict: +0.16 to +0.26 — Practical Queenside Targets with Honest Balanced Evaluation<br><br>• Our Plan — Answer 13... Qa5+ with 14. Nd2, then castle, target c5 with Nc5 or Nc4, and reroute Bh4-g3 if needed.<br><br>• Watch Out — Black gets active counterplay with ...Bb4+ or ...c5; stay ready to meet 13... Bb4+ with 14. Kf1 or sidestep ...e5 tricks.<br><br>• Key Trades & Endgame — Trading dark-squared bishops favors White once ...c5 leaves c6 weak; endgame pressure against a6 and c6 is super pleasant to play.<br><br><br>━━━ SAMPLE CONTINUATIONS ━━━<br><br>White's concrete answers to Black's forcing checks on move 13: } { [%csl Yc5,Ra6,Bc4] [%cal Ba4c5,Bd3b1,Bh4g3,Rd8a5] } ( 13. Nxa4 Qa5+ 14. Nd2 Bb4 15. Ra1 { — 95% human reply: 14. Nd2 shields cleanly and prepares O-O with Nc5/Nb3 ideas — +0.26.<br><br>And if Black checks with the bishop?: } ) ( 13. Nxa4 Bb4+ 14. Kf1 Be7 15. Kg1 { — White keeps both bishops and artificial-castles smoothly — 14. Kf1 holds +0.16, with 15. Kg1 staying solid. } ) *

